            <ArticleTitle>Resistance to activated protein C and ischemic arterial disease in a young man.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e31-3, e91-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0066-782X2010000300025</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The assessment of activated protein C resistance (APCR) caused by mutations in factor V (factor V Leiden) is an important risk factor for venous thromboembolism, of which role as the originator of arterial obstructions in situ is still a controversial subject. The clinical case of a young patient with history of coronariopathy, multiple cerebrovascular lesions and peripheral artery disease is reported. The diagnostic investigation showed APCR as the possible etiology.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
